<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">equipment</ansi>

Your character has a set of equipment slots. Use
<ansi fg="command">equip &lt;item&gt;</ansi> to wear or wield gear and
<ansi fg="command">remove &lt;item&gt;</ansi> to take it off. Use <ansi fg="command">inventory</ansi>
to see everything you have equipped and carried.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Equipment Slots ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Weapon</ansi>       Your primary weapon.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Offhand</ansi>      A shield, parrying dagger, or second weapon.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Head</ansi>         Helmets, hoods, circlets.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Neck</ansi>         Amulets, pendants, necklaces.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Shoulders</ansi>    Pauldrons, mantles, shawls.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Body</ansi>         Chest and torso armor.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Back</ansi>         Cloaks or backpacks (see below).
  <ansi fg="yellow">Belt</ansi>         Belts or bandoliers (see below).
  <ansi fg="yellow">Wrist</ansi>        Bracers and bracelets (two slots).
  <ansi fg="yellow">Gloves</ansi>       Gauntlets, gloves, wraps.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Ring</ansi>         Rings (two slots).
  <ansi fg="yellow">Legs</ansi>         Greaves, leggings, robes.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Feet</ansi>         Boots, sandals, sabatons.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Components</ansi>   A crafting component bag (see below).

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Back Slot: Cloaks vs. Backpacks ━━━</ansi>

The back slot presents a meaningful choice between defense
and carrying power.

A <ansi fg="itemname">cloak</ansi> provides armor and stat bonuses like any
other piece of gear. Good for combat-focused characters.

A <ansi fg="itemname">backpack</ansi> provides no armor, but reduces the weight
of everything inside it. If you find yourself constantly
overburdened, a backpack lets you carry significantly more
before penalties kick in. The trade-off is giving up
whatever protection a cloak would provide.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Belt Slot: Belts vs. Bandoliers ━━━</ansi>

Similar to the back slot, the belt slot has a trade-off
between armor and utility.

A <ansi fg="itemname">belt</ansi> provides armor and stat bonuses as normal.

A <ansi fg="itemname">bandolier</ansi> offers no armor but stores potions in
easy-to-reach loops. Potions you pick up are automatically
stored in the bandolier. When you <ansi fg="command">drink</ansi> a potion,
the bandolier is checked first, serving the oldest potion
of that type. This is ideal for characters who rely on
potions in combat — quick access without digging through
your pack.

Use <ansi fg="command">get &lt;potion&gt; from bandolier</ansi> to manually
retrieve a stored potion. Removing the bandolier spills
its contents into your backpack.

Bandoliers can be crafted via tailoring. See
<ansi fg="command">help leather-bandolier</ansi> for details.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Component Bag ━━━</ansi>

The component bag is a dedicated slot for crafting
materials. When equipped, materials you pick up or buy are
automatically routed into the bag instead of cluttering
your backpack. The bag has a limited capacity and reduces
the weight of its contents.

If you already have loose materials in your backpack, use
<ansi fg="command">sort</ansi> to move them into the bag. Your component bag
contents appear in a separate section of your
<ansi fg="command">inventory</ansi>.

Three tiers of bag are available, each with greater
capacity and weight reduction. See <ansi fg="command">help component-bag</ansi>
for the full list.

Removing the bag spills its contents back into your
backpack.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Mutations ━━━</ansi>

Some mutations alter your available slots:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Extra Arms</ansi>   Each level unlocks an additional arm and
               wrist slot. Extra limbs are powerful but
               draw unwanted attention and make social
               interactions more difficult.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Tail</ansi>         Replaces your legs slot with a tail slot.
               Changes how certain combat moves work.

